      "text": "To find \\(\\tan V\\) in the given right triangle \\(VWX\\), we first need to determine the lengths of the other sides and the tangent of angle \\(V\\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\)\n- \\(VW = 3\\)\n\nSince \\(VWX\\) is a right triangle with \\(VX\\) as the hypotenuse, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of the side \\(WX\\). The Pythagorean theorem states:\n\\[\nVX^2 = VW^2 + WX^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{13})^2 = 3^2 + WX^2\n\\]\n\\[\n13 = 9 + WX^2\n\\]\n\\[\nWX^2 = 13 - 9\n\\]\n\\[\nWX^2 = 4\n\\]\n\\[\nWX = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n\\]\n\nNow we have the lengths of all three sides of the triangle: \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\), \\(VW = 3\\), and \\(WX = 2\\).\n\nNext, we need to find \\(\\tan V\\). In a right triangle, the tangent of an angle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side. For angle \\(V\\), the opposite side is \\(WX\\) and the adjacent side is \\(VW\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\tan V = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}} = \\frac{WX}{VW} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\tan V\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]",
